  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong system Friday through Saturday is \nexpected to bring widespread rain with some snow over the North \nFriday/Friday night and strong winds Friday night into Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION... \nA strong low pressure system is expected to develop over the \nGreat Lakes, then move east towards the state this weekend. This\nsystem is expected to bring widespread precip to the region \nFriday. As of this update, ensemble solutions are gradually \nconverging on a track through the center of the state but there \nare still groupings of members wanting to take the track north \nand south of the area. The track through the area is relatively \nfavorable for some snowfall in northern areas as winds will have\nan easterly component, preventing substantial warm air from \nadvecting into the area. Most areas that see snow will probably \nsee less than an inch but a few areas near the canadian border \nmight reach an inch or possibly two. Snow amounts are likely to \nbe terrain dependent with higher elevations receiving the \ngreater amounts. Some slick roads possible Friday night as \ntemperatures drop to around freezing. Snow showers are likely to\npersist into Saturday with strong, gusty winds up to 40 mph. \nAlong with temperatures in the 30s this is likely to result in \nwind chills dipping into the 20s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Conditions trending drier today and tonight, with\ndry conditions areawide Thursday and Thursday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nEarly this morning, surface low pressure is tracking east along\nthe Maine coast. There is still intermittent rain throughout the\narea. Any snow in the north has turned to rain as slightly\nwarmer air continues to move in. \n\nThe rain will taper off first Downeast and Bangor region, with\nmost of the rain ending by dawn. That said, it will still be\nrather gloomy and cloudy Downeast and Bangor until perhaps mid-\nafternoon, when the low pressure center exits to the east and\nsome drier air starts working in. Further north, drying/clearing\nwill be slower, with intermittent light rain persisting through\nthe morning. Some drying this afternoon/evening, but some off\nand on light showers could persist into the evening. Also,\nremaining cloudy today in the north with low clouds, and\ntemperatures staying cool with highs only in the 40s in the\nnorth.\n\nAll areas will be dry late tonight, Thursday, and Thursday\nnight. It looks rather breezy during the day Thursday with winds\nfrom the northwest gusting 25-35 mph, but power outages not\nanticipated.",
